00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:21.890
in French [SPEAKING FRENCH] Sort
of translating, every problem

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:25.130
is millefeuille
of simple things.

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:29.510
So you should understand every
problem can be broken down

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:32.390
into many simple things.

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:36.900
And I think this is also
true for software, for code,

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.310
but also for software
architectures.

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:43.680
If you break down your big
app into simple things,

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.110
it will be simpler.

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:49.550
Until you break them
down into Microservices.

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:52.010
So that brings us
to Microservices.

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:54.620
Why Microservices?

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:58.940
Well, maybe your app
has grown too big.

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:03.080
Maybe it's actually hard
to understand, deadlocked,

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:06.590
maintain, manage.

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:10.610
This is why Microservices
can be a solution.

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:12.680
You break up the monolith.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:15.680
You decouple those
Microservices.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:18.980
You make them simpler to
understand and maintain.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:21.080
And potentially
each service will

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:23.690
be written in a
different language,

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:27.498
owned by a different team,
and deployed independently.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:34.920
As a consequence, they
will scale independently

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:36.850
if you've decoupled them.

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:39.940
The scaling is independent, too.

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:42.940
And by making them
more fine-grained,

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:46.570
that will result in a better
utilization of your resources.

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:51.100
That will result in
a lower bill for you.

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:56.250
And lastly, Microservices
pair great with Node.js

00:03:56.250 --> 00:04:00.550
due to the asychronous
nature of Node.js.

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:04.580
So Microservices
break up the monolith,

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:08.930
scale independently, and
pair great with Node.js.

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:10.160
But what is Node.js?

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:15.810
MYLES BORINS: So I
was given this slide.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:18.420
Node.js.

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:21.399
JavaScript, how many
people like JavaScript?

00:04:21.399 --> 00:04:23.850
Sweet.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.310
So I don't if any
of you have ever

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:29.100
seen this little emoji thing
before, the rocket turtle.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.510
That was the Node.js logo.

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:31.635
It was kind of like a joke.

00:04:31.635 --> 00:04:35.130
Substack drew this
turtle on a rocket ship

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:37.580
because JavaScript was not
supposed to be a fast language.

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:40.560
It was pretty amazing
how fast, both as a

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:42.450
runtime, but also for iteration.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:44.970
Node is one of those things
that just you have an idea

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:46.722
and you can get it
done in like 10 or 15

00:04:46.722 --> 00:04:48.930
minutes because someone's
already written the module.

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:49.971
You just need to find it.

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:56.220
At its base, Node is
a JavaScript runtime.

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:57.990
We take the V8
virtual machine that

00:04:57.990 --> 00:04:59.880
comes in the Chrome
browser, we wrap it

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:02.850
with libuv, which is an
underlying architecture

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:05.100
wrapping all the system calls.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:07.410
And that kind of abstracts
all the different platforms

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:10.860
and allows developers to
just write JavaScript and be

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:14.040
able to do compute,
write servers,

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:16.330
and all those different things.

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:18.510
It allows you to leverage
your front end skills.

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:19.950
It's event driven.

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:23.660
What that means is that
compute is driven by events.

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:25.410
Something computes
when something happens.

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:27.780
That can be an HTTP request.

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:31.710
That can be some sort of
message coming over a socket.

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:34.020
It can be user input
with a keyboard.

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:37.200
It can be some sensor if
you're working with IoT.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:39.480
Any form of I/O.
So anytime there's

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:41.340
input, some sort
of compute runs.

00:05:43.807 --> 00:05:45.390
The [? adventure ?]
of an architecture

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:50.110
plays really great with async
non-blocking I/O. Multiple

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:53.470
things can run in parallel
without blocking each other.

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:56.200
When I make some sort of
request over a socket,

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:58.420
I'm able to suspend, let
other things execute,

00:05:58.420 --> 00:06:02.492
and resume when I'm done
without requiring the developer

00:06:02.492 --> 00:06:03.450
to program any of this.

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:04.750
It just happens.

00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:07.030
Many events execute without
blocking each other.

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:09.581
And this ends up being
really great for web servers

00:06:09.581 --> 00:06:11.080
because it means
multiple people can

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:14.080
connect to the same
process at the same time

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:15.220
and it just works.

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:16.840
You don't have to
worry about mutex.

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:19.520
You don't have to
worry about timing.

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:22.320
You just worry about
handling the events.

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:27.800
So to sum that up, Node.js
is JavaScript runtime

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:33.270
that's event driven with
async non-blocking I/O.

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:34.860
STEREN GIANNINI:
And the last term

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:37.770
that we need to define
from the title of this talk

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:38.940
is serverless.

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:41.010
So who here is using
a serverless product?

00:06:44.010 --> 00:06:47.730
So we should agree that there
are a lot of definitions

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:49.170
for serverless.

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:51.960
I'm going to highlight here
the key characteristics

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:55.040
that we use along this talk.

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:58.180
The first one that, to me,
is the main characteristic

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:02.210
is that you pay
for what you use.

00:07:02.210 --> 00:07:04.030
So it's a billing model.

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:07.210
If you don't use
it, you don't pay,

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:11.860
and then the bill is
proportional to your usage.

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:14.110
Of course you don't need
to provision in advance

00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:18.970
and your resource, all of
this, is done on demand.

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:23.080
So why are we able to
achieve that billing model?

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:26.280
Because of an operational model.

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:31.050
So a serverless product
will scale with usage

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:34.000
without having to
worry about it.

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:37.650
It grows with you from a
single developer to a company

00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:40.620
with thousands of developers.

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:43.650
It follows your usage.

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:45.990
And why can it scale?

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:49.900
Because of a programming model.

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:54.510
The cloud providers
takes care of the rest.

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:56.770
You focus on your code.

00:07:56.770 --> 00:07:59.970
That means that no
infrastructure management,

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:01.080
no VM management.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:03.690
Everything is
abstracted from you.

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:04.920
So you focus on your code.

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:07.980
That also means that you focus
on delivering business value

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:11.120
and value to your users.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:15.800
So to recap, serverless,
pay for what you use,

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:18.700
it scales and allows
you to focus on code.

00:08:23.350 --> 00:08:25.750
These are the principles we
have to use along that talk

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:29.470
and you will see it's not
actually not only for compute.

00:08:31.914 --> 00:08:33.289
MYLES BORINS: So
Google Cloud has

00:08:33.289 --> 00:08:35.990
a number of different
serverless offerings.

00:08:35.990 --> 00:08:37.150
We have Cloud Functions.

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:39.650
How many people here have been
using Node in Cloud Functions

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:40.843
so far?

00:08:40.843 --> 00:08:42.049
Cool.

00:08:42.049 --> 00:08:44.330
We've got App Engine
Standard, we've

00:08:44.330 --> 00:08:50.140
got Data Store, Cloud storage,
BigQuery Pub/Sub, Dataflow,

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:54.380
ML Engine, the Firebase
suite, as well as AppScripts.

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:56.290
And all of these
different products,

00:08:56.290 --> 00:08:59.170
what defines them as serverless
and makes them all serverless

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:01.266
is the fact that you
don't have to manage them.

00:09:01.266 --> 00:09:02.390
We will scale them for you.

00:09:02.390 --> 00:09:04.220
We will manage them for you.

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:06.590
You can utilize them
as much as you need it.

