dive into three updates

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:36.930
pieces of our infrastructure.

00:13:36.930 --> 00:13:40.110
The first one that is
what we call internally

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:42.210
faster than light builds.

00:13:42.210 --> 00:13:44.430
So what's that?

00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:47.510
When you type gcloud
app deploy, we

00:13:47.510 --> 00:13:50.970
will first only upload the
files that have actually

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:52.960
changed since last time.

00:13:52.960 --> 00:13:56.110
This is an optimization
for the upload process.

00:13:56.110 --> 00:13:59.080
The faster than light
build arrives after.

00:13:59.080 --> 00:14:01.840
So what we do is we look
at your package.json

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:06.810
and package-lock.json to install
your dependencies in the Cloud.

00:14:06.810 --> 00:14:10.320
So we do npm install
for you in the cloud.

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:12.540
Good.

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:15.600
So this is what we defined
as the speed of light.

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:18.600
But actually, if we notice
that your package.json

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:21.440
and your dependencies
haven't changed, well,

00:14:21.440 --> 00:14:24.420
what we are going to
do is just give you

00:14:24.420 --> 00:14:27.670
what we just gave you before
if you do a new deployment.

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:33.120
So we use our caching system
to give user cache to modules.

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:38.100
And that's how we can
go faster than light.

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:40.290
This was the first piece
of data infrastructure

00:14:40.290 --> 00:14:41.580
for this new runtime.

00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:46.540
The second one is a new
runtime environment.

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:49.440
So let's hear a bit of
the technical stack.

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:54.330
So first, you gave us your code
when you did gcloud app deploy.

00:14:54.330 --> 00:14:57.100
So this is your
application code.

00:14:57.100 --> 00:15:01.395
As I said, we installed
the Node modules for you.

00:15:01.395 --> 00:15:05.020
So including the native modules,
they can be any modules.

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:05.970
Good.

00:15:05.970 --> 00:15:09.080
Under that, of course,
there is Node.js.

00:15:09.080 --> 00:15:12.090
And here, this is an
off-the-shelf Node.js.

00:15:12.090 --> 00:15:15.460
There's no special
customization here.

00:15:15.460 --> 00:15:18.900
Node is relying on
the OS packages.

00:15:18.900 --> 00:15:22.000
And for example, you might have
heard about Headless Chrome.

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:25.860
And yes, all of the packages
needed to run Headless Chrome

00:15:25.860 --> 00:15:28.530
are present in the
App Engine image.

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:35.120
And that lives on top of a
regular OS, which is Ubuntu.

00:15:35.120 --> 00:15:37.140
The last three pieces--

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:42.010
Node, OS packages, and Ubuntu--

00:15:42.010 --> 00:15:44.620
are automatically
updated without you

00:15:44.620 --> 00:15:46.390
having to worry about it.

00:15:46.390 --> 00:15:50.340
So you'll automatically
update your Node.js version,

00:15:50.340 --> 00:15:53.660
the minor and patch
version of the Node.js.

00:15:53.660 --> 00:15:57.270
The OS package and the OS is
also automatically updated.

00:15:57.270 --> 00:16:01.470
All of that lives in
a secured sandbox.

00:16:01.470 --> 00:16:03.330
And this is a new
sandbox technology

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:06.060
that we are introducing
with this new runtime.

00:16:06.060 --> 00:16:10.150
Under the hood, the sandbox
is based on G Vysor.

00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:12.930
So G Vysor is a
content runtime sandbox

00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:14.800
that we open sourced last week.

00:16:14.800 --> 00:16:17.090
Check it out.

00:16:17.090 --> 00:16:21.181
This is our new
runtime environment.

00:16:21.181 --> 00:16:22.680
And the last piece
of infrastructure

00:16:22.680 --> 00:16:26.080
I would like to highlight
is automatic scaling.

00:16:26.080 --> 00:16:31.140
So what is automatic scaling?

00:16:31.140 --> 00:16:32.930
Let's take an example.

00:16:32.930 --> 00:16:37.510
So App Engine constantly
monitors the instance capacity

00:16:37.510 --> 00:16:40.620
and traffic, your application
capacity and traffic.

00:16:40.620 --> 00:16:44.560
And it ensures that the
appropriate number of instances

00:16:44.560 --> 00:16:47.660
are available to run
your application.

00:16:47.660 --> 00:16:48.620
So what does that mean?

00:16:48.620 --> 00:16:50.150
Let's take an example.

00:16:50.150 --> 00:16:52.450
So as I said, if there
is no request arriving

00:16:52.450 --> 00:16:57.280
to your application, there
is no instance scheduled.

00:16:57.280 --> 00:16:59.600
And when I say instance,
it's not a VM instance,

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:03.140
it's an abstract that
we named instance.

00:17:03.140 --> 00:17:07.339
OK, now some traffic
arrives to your application.

00:17:07.339 --> 00:17:13.119
Well, we will scale up as
fast as possible one instance.

00:17:13.119 --> 00:17:15.250
Here I'm going to
pause for a second.

00:17:15.250 --> 00:17:17.230
I want to highlight
one concept that is

00:17:17.230 --> 00:17:19.900
very important for developers.

00:17:19.900 --> 00:17:22.450
The very first of
these requests really

00:17:22.450 --> 00:17:26.829
hits what we call a cold start.

00:17:26.829 --> 00:17:31.730
That means that this request
will actually hit the instance

00:17:31.730 --> 00:17:34.060
while it is starting up.

00:17:34.060 --> 00:17:38.030
And this usually results in
a higher latency than usual.

00:17:38.030 --> 00:17:42.100
Of course we try to not
retry, but we do boot up

00:17:42.100 --> 00:17:44.330
the instance very, very fast.

00:17:44.330 --> 00:17:47.200
But you also have a
responsibility here.

00:17:47.200 --> 00:17:51.760
You have to ensure that your
start command is fast too.

00:17:51.760 --> 00:17:54.910
So in the start script of
your package [INAUDIBLE],,

00:17:54.910 --> 00:17:57.010
make sure to not do
things like build

00:17:57.010 --> 00:17:59.110
or a lot of initialization.

00:17:59.110 --> 00:18:02.260
Do the minimum required
to start your application,

00:18:02.260 --> 00:18:05.080
because this will have an
impact on the cold start

00:18:05.080 --> 00:18:07.040
of your application.

00:18:07.040 --> 00:18:09.190
OK, so here as I said,
the very first request

00:18:09.190 --> 00:18:10.630
will hit this cold start.

00:18:10.630 --> 00:18:14.110
But the next ones,
everything is fine.

00:18:14.110 --> 00:18:17.950
But then your traffic will
start to increase a little bit.

00:18:17.950 --> 00:18:22.634
And here, before this first
instance arrives at capacity,

00:18:22.634 --> 00:18:24.550
we will anticipate that
you need a second one.

00:18:24.550 --> 00:18:27.370
And we will actually
schedule a second one.

00:18:27.370 --> 00:18:30.430
So that when you actually
need it, it's there.

00:18:30.430 --> 00:18:33.550
Doesn't need to scale more.

00:18:33.550 --> 00:18:38.200
And now let's imagine your app
is featured on a popular blog

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:40.840
post and you have a
huge spike of traffic.

00:18:40.840 --> 00:18:41.940
Well, don't worry.

00:18:41.940 --> 00:18:44.420
App Engine scales very fast.

00:18:44.420 --> 00:18:48.460
So it would be to scale up
instances as much as needed.

00:18:48.460 --> 00:18:51.940
You control, you can tell us a
maximum of number of instances

00:18:51.940 --> 00:18:54.290
if that matters to you.

00:18:54.290 --> 00:18:57.790
And of course, when
things go down,

00:18:57.790 --> 00:19:02.470
well we will ramp down the
number of instances to zero.

00:19:02.470 --> 00:19:04.780
So that's what we call
automatic scaling.

00:19:04.780 --> 00:19:09.150
You did not have to do
anything to get this behavior.

00:19:09.150 --> 00:19:11.700
But this is not new at all.

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:15.850
App Engine has been doing that
for 10 years, 10 years now.

00:19:15.850 --> 00:19:20.670
What is new is that actually we
updated our automatic scaling

00:19:20.670 --> 00:19:23.230
algorithm.

00:19:23.230 --> 00:19:27.010
So the updates include
it is more predictable.

00:19:27.010 --> 00:19:30.370
As a developer, you will better
understand how it behaves.

00:19:30.370 --> 00:19:32.350
When you look at
your instance chart,

00:19:32.350 --> 00:19:35.200
you will better understand it.

00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:39.420
Second, it actually improves
the instance utilization

00:19:39.420 --> 00:19:41.020
and performance.

00:19:41.020 --> 00:19:46.620
So we measured a reduction
of 30% of those cold starts.

00:19:46.620 --> 00:19:48.960
So that means that the
detail, the latency

00:19:48.960 --> 00:19:54.100
of all of our apps across all
of App Engine has went down.

00:19:54.100 --> 00:19:57.700
And also better utilization
means lower bills

00:19:57.700 --> 00:19:59.950
for everybody using App Engine.

00:19:59.950 --> 00:20:04.440
And this was released
in the last months.

00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:07.170
The third point are
new settings that we

00:20:07.170 --> 00:20:10.300
are introducing to better
control this automatic scaling

00:20:10.300 --> 00:20:11.130
algorithms.

00:20:11.130 --> 00:20:13.030
Those new settings
help you balance

00:20:13.030 --> 00:20:16.150
between cost and performance.

00:20:16.150 --> 00:20:19.180
Also, as I said, help you
cap the number of instances

00:20:19.180 --> 00:20:23.989
so that you can cap your bill
but also the number of database

00:20:23.989 --> 00:20:25.030
connections, for example.

00:20:27.820 --> 00:20:31.030
So this was three pieces of
infrastructure we updated.

00:20:31.030 --> 00:20:32.390
Up to you, Myles.

00:20:32.390 --> 00:20:34.330
MYLES BORINS: So
we built a product

00:20:34.330 --> 00:20:37.090
with Node.js comprised
of Microservices

00:20:37.090 --> 00:20:40.030
to share with you today.

00:20:40.030 --> 00:20:42.620
And Steren's going to demo it
on the machine for us right now.

00:20:51.890 --> 00:20:54.650
STEREN GIANNINI: All
right, so go for it.

00:20:54.650 --> 00:20:57.020
MYLES BORINS: So we
created a web page watcher.

00:20:57.020 --> 00:21:00.380
And we're able to put an
address into the submission bar

00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:02.090
and it will start watching it.

00:21:02.090 --> 00:21:03.830
And at five-minute
increments, it's

00:21:03.830 --> 00:21:06.020
going to use Headless
Chrome to take a screen

00:21:06.020 --> 00:21:07.250
capture of the website.

00:21:07.250 --> 00:21:09.520
And it's going to compare
it to other screen captures.

00:21:09.520 --> 00:21:11.660
And it's going to only
take screen captures

00:21:11.660 --> 00:21:14.460
when there's actually been
a difference in the page.

00:21:14.460 --> 00:21:17.630
This website allows
you to set any number

00:21:17.630 --> 00:21:19.600
of different websites to watch.

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:22.550
And this has been a lot of fun
to use on the Google website

00:21:22.550 --> 00:21:26.420
to see the changes
to the Google header.

00:21:26.420 --> 00:21:29.357
You could use it to watch
your own site to make sure

00:21:29.357 --> 00:21:31.940
that things aren't going down
if you have a personal page that

00:21:31.940 --> 00:21:34.170
isn't changing a lot.

00:21:34.170 --> 00:21:38.870
But it was a way for us to
experiment with breaking things

00:21:38.870 --> 00:21:40.970
into different services,
and put together

00:21:40.970 --> 00:21:43.460
a really great example.

00:21:43.460 --> 00:21:46.267
So Steren, did you have anything
you wanted to add to that?

00:21:46.267 --> 00:21:48.350
STEREN GIANNINI: I want
to tell you how it's done.

00:21:48.350 --> 00:21:49.260
MYLES BORINS: That sounds great.

00:21:49.260 --> 00:21:51.176
STEREN GIANNINI: Let's
get back to the slides.

00:21:52.890 --> 00:21:53.880
So this is our product.

00:21:53.880 --> 00:21:56.990
This is our application
the website watcher.

00:21:56.990 --> 00:22:00.660
How did we build it?

00:22:00.660 --> 00:22:05.840
Well, every five
minutes there is

00:22:05.840 --> 00:22:09.900
a cron job that's we trigger
of a first Microservices.

00:22:09.900 --> 00:22:13.550
This Microservice is
called Task Scheduler.

00:22:13.550 --> 00:22:18.650
The job of this Microservice is
to ask our database for a list

00:22:18.650 --> 00:22:23.030
of web pages to screenshot.

00:22:23.030 --> 00:22:27.050
When it receives this list,
it will actually [? enqueue ?]

00:22:27.050 --> 00:22:32.900
into a Pub/Sub topic a message
for each page to screenshot.

00:22:32.900 --> 00:22:34.940
This is the first Microservice.

00:22:34.940 --> 00:22:39.260
Then Pub/Sub will dispatch,
will push to a second

00:22:39.260 --> 00:22:43.910
Microservice, the web pages.

00:22:43.910 --> 00:22:45.710
And the job of
this Microservice,

00:22:45.710 --> 00:22:48.900
is it has one job, is
to take screenshots.

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:51.500
So as I said previously,
this is using

00:22:51.500 --> 00:22:53.530
Headless Chrome under
the hood, [INAUDIBLE]

00:22:53.530 --> 00:22:56.630
to take the screenshots.

00:22:56.630 --> 00:22:59.870
So it opens a web page,
takes a screenshot,

00:22:59.870 --> 00:23:02.570
saves it into Google
Cloud Storage.

00:23:02.570 --> 00:23:04.520
That's its only job.

00:23:04.520 --> 00:23:08.375
Now a new file arrives
into Cloud Storage.

00:23:11.600 --> 00:23:15.230
This will send an event that
will trigger a Google Cloud

00:23:15.230 --> 00:23:19.190
function that will compare
this new screenshot

00:23:19.190 --> 00:23:22.670
with the last known reference
screenshot for that web page.

00:23:22.670 --> 00:23:25.730
We call it image diff because
the job of that function

00:23:25.730 --> 00:23:27.410
is to compare two images.

00:23:27.410 --> 00:23:30.080
And if there is a difference
between the images,

00:23:30.080 --> 00:23:34.100
it will store the new image into
a new folder in Google Cloud

00:23:34.100 --> 00:23:35.400
Storage.

00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:38.360
So this is how we
continuously get

00:23:38.360 --> 00:23:43.730
the updates of all of the web
pages that we want to track.

00:23:43.730 --> 00:23:48.580
OK now, a user arrives
onto a website.

00:23:48.580 --> 00:23:51.980
So an HTP request arrives
onto our web Frontend,

00:23:51.980 --> 00:23:56.480
another Microcservice, that
will query our database asking

00:23:56.480 --> 00:23:57.740
for a list of web pages.

00:23:57.740 --> 00:23:59.330
You've seen that list.

00:23:59.330 --> 00:24:02.060
And when I see the detail
of one of those web pages,

00:24:02.060 --> 00:24:03.800
we actually display
the screenshots.

00:24:03.800 --> 00:24:06.690
So it also queries
Cloud Storage.

00:24:06.690 --> 00:24:09.020
So that's how we
build our products.

00:24:09.020 --> 00:24:14.400
Four custom Microservices all
communicating with each other.

00:24:14.400 --> 00:24:19.500
And now, Myles is going to
tell you more about the code.

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:22.040
MYLES BORINS: So the first
Microservice that we're showing

00:24:22.040 --> 00:24:24.847
is our screenshot server.

00:24:24.847 --> 00:24:26.930
You're going to notice
we're using async functions

00:24:26.930 --> 00:24:29.330
because App Engine
standard comes

00:24:29.330 --> 00:24:33.020
with Node 8 out of the box.

00:24:33.020 --> 00:24:35.990
We're going to start a browser
session by awaiting Puppeteer

00:24:35.990 --> 00:24:37.160
to launch.

00:24:37.160 --> 00:24:41.660
We're going to then await a new
page from the browser object.

00:24:41.660 --> 00:24:44.430
We're going to await
surfing to that URL.

00:24:44.430 --> 00:24:48.380
We'll await setting the viewport
to the size of the image

00:24:48.380 --> 00:24:49.610
that we want to take.

00:24:49.610 --> 00:24:52.730
And then we'll await a
screenshot in the image buffer.

00:24:52.730 --> 00:24:55.440
We then await
closing the browser.

00:24:55.440 --> 00:24:57.350
And then we return
the image buffer.

00:24:57.350 --> 00:24:59.690
We're doing a lot of
asynchronous work in here

00:24:59.690 --> 00:25:02.720
that is going to allow us to
use the same service to pull

00:25:02.720 --> 00:25:04.700
lots of different images
at the exact same time

00:25:04.700 --> 00:25:05.750
without blocking.

00:25:05.750 --> 00:25:08.240
We're able to do it in
a single linear fashion

00:25:08.240 --> 00:25:10.730
without having to manage
a whole bunch of callbacks

00:25:10.730 --> 00:25:12.369
or any sorts of promises.

00:25:12.369 --> 00:25:13.910
Although under the
hood, there's lots

00:25:13.910 --> 00:25:15.650
of different promises going on.

00:25:15.650 --> 00:25:20.750
Now, this is deployed
using App Engine standard.

00:25:20.750 --> 00:25:22.820
And there were some
other talks going

00:25:22.820 --> 00:25:25.190
on this week about
Headless Chrome.

00:25:25.190 --> 00:25:27.230
And you can find those
if you go and check

00:25:27.230 --> 00:25:29.100
the I/O webpage later.

00:25:29.100 --> 00:25:33.170
So this is the part
of the service that

00:25:33.170 --> 00:25:34.790
actually saves to a bucket.

00:25:34.790 --> 00:25:37.310
Similarly we're
using Async/Await

00:25:37.310 --> 00:25:39.750
to be able to do this
in a linear fashion.

00:25:39.750 --> 00:25:42.530
But we go to the
bucket, we rename

00:25:42.530 --> 00:25:45.650
the images to be
based on a time stamp,

00:25:45.650 --> 00:25:50.240
and we save it, and await that
information, and then log it.

00:25:50.240 --> 00:25:53.720
And because all of the Node.js
client libraries support

00:25:53.720 --> 00:25:56.540
promises, you're going to be
able to use our various client

00:25:56.540 --> 00:25:58.580
libraries to talk to all
of our various servers

00:25:58.580 --> 00:26:00.770
using Async/Await
right out of the box.

00:26:04.447 --> 00:26:06.030
And then the last
thing that we wanted

00:26:06.030 --> 00:26:10.520
to show you was the image
diff from our Cloud Function

00:26:10.520 --> 00:26:12.590
that's doing the image diff.

00:26:12.590 --> 00:26:17.040
Every time a new file gets
added to a Cloud Storage bucket,

00:26:17.040 --> 00:26:18.920
this function's
automatically called.

00:26:18.920 --> 00:26:21.500
And what we wanted to
just show is how quickly

00:26:21.500 --> 00:26:24.410
from the event that the
function is executed with

00:26:24.410 --> 00:26:28.190
we can grab the file
and the context at which

00:26:28.190 --> 00:26:29.930
of that file inside
of Cloud Storage.

00:26:29.930 --> 00:26:32.860
So we don't even need
to write any logic

00:26:32.860 --> 00:26:33.860
to tie the two together.

00:26:33.860 --> 00:26:35.840
We just use one
service to save it,

00:26:35.840 --> 00:26:37.379
and this new
service just reacts.

00:26:37.379 --> 00:26:39.170
And one thing that's
really great about all

00:26:39.170 --> 00:26:41.600
these different services
is that out of the box,

00:26:41.600 --> 00:26:43.240
if you start logging,
Stackdriver's

00:26:43.240 --> 00:26:44.990
going to grab all of
those logs and you're

00:26:44.990 --> 00:26:48.452
going to be able to use
Stackdriver logging to look

00:26:48.452 --> 00:26:49.910
at all the logs of
what's going on.

00:26:49.910 --> 00:26:52.451
And I don't know about you, but
I never write a service right

00:26:52.451 --> 00:26:53.450
at the first sign.

00:26:53.450 --> 00:26:56.120
I write it locally, and then it
works differently in the cloud.

00:26:56.120 --> 00:27:00.310
Being able to just log and have
it working is really awesome.

00:27:00.310 --> 00:27:02.120
For the demo that
we put together,

00:27:02.120 --> 00:27:05.420
you'll see online
later we used Bunyan

00:27:05.420 --> 00:27:07.970
and have a pretty
robust logging system

00:27:07.970 --> 00:27:10.885
that you'll be able
to take a look at.

