STEREN GIANNINI: So let's
look at the Cloud Console

00:27:13.010 --> 00:27:16.800
to see those Microservices
in the console.

00:27:16.800 --> 00:27:20.510
So as I said, we do have
three App Engine services.

00:27:20.510 --> 00:27:23.880
So you will see them on
the App Engine dashboard.

00:27:23.880 --> 00:27:27.260
So opening my services-- or
we have four because I just

00:27:27.260 --> 00:27:29.690
deployed one earlier with you.

00:27:29.690 --> 00:27:32.420
So defaults,
screenshot, task editor.

00:27:32.420 --> 00:27:35.220
We have one cloud function.

00:27:35.220 --> 00:27:37.345
So if I open it,

00:27:43.520 --> 00:27:44.800
it will display here.

00:27:44.800 --> 00:27:46.690
Image diff, as you
see, the trigger

00:27:46.690 --> 00:27:50.830
is a Cloud Storage
bucket trigger.

00:27:50.830 --> 00:27:54.810
Now, as Myles said, out of
the box all of your logs

00:27:54.810 --> 00:27:58.030
are going to be collected
into Stackdriver logging.

00:27:58.030 --> 00:28:03.610
So here, let me filter
out some of those logs

00:28:03.610 --> 00:28:09.458
and only show the application
logs that are here.

00:28:13.750 --> 00:28:17.680
So these are the logs for our
app across all of our services.

00:28:17.680 --> 00:28:19.650
So as you see, we see
we loaded the website.

00:28:25.910 --> 00:28:27.730
We save screenshots
so all of this

00:28:27.730 --> 00:28:29.940
gets logged, and with
a different severity.

00:28:29.940 --> 00:28:32.932
Here we had an
error, apparently.

00:28:32.932 --> 00:28:34.390
And the last thing
I wanted to show

00:28:34.390 --> 00:28:36.800
is that when your logs
go to Stackdriver,

00:28:36.800 --> 00:28:39.580
they are going to be
collected and grouped together

00:28:39.580 --> 00:28:42.010
by Stackdriver Error Reporting.

00:28:42.010 --> 00:28:45.350
So what you see here is
a list of error groups.

00:28:45.350 --> 00:28:47.230
So all of your errors,
if they are the same,

00:28:47.230 --> 00:28:50.630
are going to be grouped together
in this place as one group.

00:28:50.630 --> 00:28:55.220
So this is out of the box when
you use App Engine, especially

00:28:55.220 --> 00:28:57.860
Node.js App Engine.

00:28:57.860 --> 00:29:02.534
That is all for the
demo of the resources.

00:29:02.534 --> 00:29:03.700
Let's go back to the slides.

00:29:06.240 --> 00:29:08.220
And now I'm going to
talk a little bit more

00:29:08.220 --> 00:29:12.250
about how to connect
Microservices.

00:29:12.250 --> 00:29:16.860
So of course, you can
directly call a service.

00:29:16.860 --> 00:29:19.430
So that's the case
for example, when

00:29:19.430 --> 00:29:21.610
a client calls our front end.

00:29:21.610 --> 00:29:24.740
So an HTP request arrives,
that's a direct call.

00:29:24.740 --> 00:29:26.930
It's also the case when
you receive a webhook.

00:29:26.930 --> 00:29:30.380
When you receive a webhook,
that would be a direct call.

00:29:30.380 --> 00:29:34.080
And lastly, two Microservices
can call each other directly,

00:29:34.080 --> 00:29:34.580
of course.

00:29:37.140 --> 00:29:40.320
Another kind of communication
between Microservices,

00:29:40.320 --> 00:29:43.320
which actually can be quite
convenient and useful,

00:29:43.320 --> 00:29:47.680
is what we call asynchronous
execution and events.

00:29:47.680 --> 00:29:52.440
So if you remember, every five
minutes [INAUDIBLE] services

00:29:52.440 --> 00:29:54.090
was scheduled.

00:29:54.090 --> 00:29:57.240
So this is one kind
of scheduled services.

00:29:59.990 --> 00:30:04.980
Now you remember any time we
put a file into Cloud Storage,

00:30:04.980 --> 00:30:05.880
something happened.

00:30:05.880 --> 00:30:07.620
The function was triggered.

00:30:07.620 --> 00:30:10.650
So this is another
kind of communication.

00:30:10.650 --> 00:30:13.140
We call that reactive services.

00:30:13.140 --> 00:30:16.270
So when something
happens, this is

00:30:16.270 --> 00:30:20.940
triggered [INAUDIBLE] events.

00:30:20.940 --> 00:30:22.970
Another way to do an
asychronous execution

00:30:22.970 --> 00:30:26.280
is to actually use a task queue.

00:30:26.280 --> 00:30:32.300
So one service will make
you a task into a queue.

00:30:32.300 --> 00:30:34.910
And then this queue
will make sure

00:30:34.910 --> 00:30:38.750
that this task is executed
by retrying, if needed,

00:30:38.750 --> 00:30:41.420
and by dispatching at
the rate that you want.

00:30:41.420 --> 00:30:44.300
So task queues can be
quite convenient for

00:30:44.300 --> 00:30:47.180
asynchronous task execution.

00:30:47.180 --> 00:30:50.860
And lastly, as we
made in our demo,

00:30:50.860 --> 00:30:54.740
you can use a publisher
and subscriber

00:30:54.740 --> 00:30:57.280
pattern when one
service will publish

00:30:57.280 --> 00:31:02.890
a message into a Pub/Sub topic,
and one or many other services

00:31:02.890 --> 00:31:05.060
that are subscribed
to that topic,

00:31:05.060 --> 00:31:09.380
will receive the message.

00:31:09.380 --> 00:31:12.020
And lastly, maybe
this is obvious,

00:31:12.020 --> 00:31:14.360
but sharing storage
is also a way

00:31:14.360 --> 00:31:16.460
to communicate between services.

00:31:16.460 --> 00:31:19.340
You can have two services
sharing the same database.

00:31:19.340 --> 00:31:21.140
That is a kind of connection.

00:31:21.140 --> 00:31:23.750
In our case, we used Cloud
Datastore and Google Cloud

00:31:23.750 --> 00:31:25.400
Storage.

00:31:25.400 --> 00:31:29.210
So connecting Microservices,
you can directly call them,

00:31:29.210 --> 00:31:31.580
you can use asynchronous
execution and events,

00:31:31.580 --> 00:31:32.750
and shared storage.

00:31:35.730 --> 00:31:37.830
MYLES BORINS: So
after kind of talking

00:31:37.830 --> 00:31:41.040
about all these different
types of services and products,

00:31:41.040 --> 00:31:43.800
you may be wondering when
should I use functions?

00:31:43.800 --> 00:31:46.020
Or when should I use App Engine?

00:31:46.020 --> 00:31:49.350
Or maybe when should
I use a Kubernetes?

00:31:49.350 --> 00:31:51.760
These decisions are things
that teams need to make.

00:31:51.760 --> 00:31:55.490
So I want to get a little bit
more into functions first.

00:31:55.490 --> 00:31:57.610
So we talked about
events and async

00:31:57.610 --> 00:31:59.400
and that's a lot of
the programming model

00:31:59.400 --> 00:32:00.275
that's used for that.

00:32:00.275 --> 00:32:02.540
So you have different
kinds of triggers.

00:32:02.540 --> 00:32:04.994
You have Pub/Sub, you
have Google Cloud Storage.

00:32:04.994 --> 00:32:07.410
So whenever something ends up
in a bucket, it triggers it.

00:32:07.410 --> 00:32:09.510
You can have database
transactions.

00:32:09.510 --> 00:32:12.002
One that's really
interesting is logs.

00:32:12.002 --> 00:32:13.710
So you can have a
function run every time

00:32:13.710 --> 00:32:15.074
you see things in logs.

00:32:15.074 --> 00:32:17.490
You can use that to filter
certain kinds of error messages

00:32:17.490 --> 00:32:21.660
or automate pager duty or
something fun like that.

00:32:21.660 --> 00:32:23.670
Maybe not pager duty.

00:32:23.670 --> 00:32:26.250
But you can also use webhooks.

00:32:26.250 --> 00:32:28.397
Any sort of HTTP
can go and call in.

00:32:28.397 --> 00:32:30.480
One of the things that's
nice with Cloud Functions

00:32:30.480 --> 00:32:33.220
is they can be really
single-purpose.

00:32:33.220 --> 00:32:35.729
It has a well-defined
programming model

00:32:35.729 --> 00:32:37.770
with a really quick way
to get to the data that's

00:32:37.770 --> 00:32:39.450
coming in from the event.

00:32:39.450 --> 00:32:43.210
One that I really like is
an emailer, for example.

00:32:43.210 --> 00:32:45.390
So you may have a whole
bunch of different services

00:32:45.390 --> 00:32:46.920
that need to send email.

00:32:46.920 --> 00:32:48.570
So if you have one
Cloud Function that

00:32:48.570 --> 00:32:50.510
just does all the auth
and all the message

00:32:50.510 --> 00:32:52.890
handling to whatever email
service you're running,

00:32:52.890 --> 00:32:57.560
you can make a email topic on
Pub/Sub and just publish a to,

00:32:57.560 --> 00:33:01.230
a from, a body, and a title,
and an email will just go out.

00:33:01.230 --> 00:33:03.840
And let's say you decide to
change your email provider

00:33:03.840 --> 00:33:04.450
service.

00:33:04.450 --> 00:33:06.450
You just have to change
that one Cloud Function.

00:33:06.450 --> 00:33:08.760
And no one else needs
to refactor their code.

00:33:08.760 --> 00:33:12.540
As long as you have a defined
API surface between all

00:33:12.540 --> 00:33:14.780
your services that
isn't broken, you

00:33:14.780 --> 00:33:19.270
could change it to any language
or any service you want to.

00:33:19.270 --> 00:33:22.350
So on the other hand,
we have App Engine.

00:33:22.350 --> 00:33:26.070
App Engine, for
example, HTTP services

00:33:26.070 --> 00:33:29.370
are generally what are exposed.

00:33:29.370 --> 00:33:31.107
Now, it's multi-purpose, though.

00:33:31.107 --> 00:33:32.940
You could use it for
front end applications.

00:33:32.940 --> 00:33:35.830
You can use it to
serve static resources.

00:33:35.830 --> 00:33:38.550
It does not have a
defined function wrapper.

00:33:38.550 --> 00:33:41.790
So you can just take any Node
application for the most part

00:33:41.790 --> 00:33:44.460
that you've already been
running and just launch it.

00:33:44.460 --> 00:33:46.830
If you have multiple
different things that

00:33:46.830 --> 00:33:49.230
are going, multiple routes
that you're handling,

00:33:49.230 --> 00:33:52.140
database queries
templating, you may

00:33:52.140 --> 00:33:54.720
find that running
something on App Engine

00:33:54.720 --> 00:33:57.420
makes more sense for the use
case than a Cloud Function.

00:34:00.130 --> 00:34:04.030
But you can define your
own compute relationship.

00:34:04.030 --> 00:34:07.390
Card functions and App Engine
work really great together.

00:34:07.390 --> 00:34:10.270
If your team prefers thinking
about things as functions,

00:34:10.270 --> 00:34:10.870
run with that.

00:34:10.870 --> 00:34:15.170
I know entire websites that
have thousands of page views

00:34:15.170 --> 00:34:18.580
and hours and they've written
their entire infrastructure

00:34:18.580 --> 00:34:20.010
on functions.

00:34:20.010 --> 00:34:21.670
And there's tons
of big note apps

00:34:21.670 --> 00:34:25.553
that could just run on an App
Engine and scale as necessary.

00:34:25.553 --> 00:34:27.219
You could really break
apart the problem

00:34:27.219 --> 00:34:30.139
as it makes sense to your
team as you need to go.

00:34:30.139 --> 00:34:33.050
And they both share a
lot of similarities.

00:34:33.050 --> 00:34:34.630
They're both going
to scale to zero

00:34:34.630 --> 00:34:35.754
when you're not using them.

00:34:35.754 --> 00:34:37.600
You're going to pay
for what you use.

00:34:37.600 --> 00:34:40.070
And under the hood, they're
using the same sandbox.

00:34:40.070 --> 00:34:43.360
They're using the
same auto scaling.

00:34:43.360 --> 00:34:46.389
There's no right or wrong.

00:34:46.389 --> 00:34:48.199
One of the cool
things about it, too,

00:34:48.199 --> 00:34:49.967
is you can start thinking--

00:34:49.967 --> 00:34:51.550
it almost becomes
more of a difference

00:34:51.550 --> 00:34:53.341
of how you want to
think about your compute

00:34:53.341 --> 00:34:55.739
and how you want to
factor your code.

00:34:55.739 --> 00:34:58.300
But from an OPS perspective,
they're very similar.

00:34:58.300 --> 00:34:59.980
And we also wanted
to announce today

00:34:59.980 --> 00:35:02.909
that [INAUDIBLE] is going to
be coming to GCF really soon.

00:35:02.909 --> 00:35:05.200
So you're going to be able
to use all of these features

00:35:05.200 --> 00:35:08.075
both on Cloud Functions
as well as App Engine.

00:35:10.810 --> 00:35:13.390
STEREN GIANNINI:
All right, to recap,

00:35:13.390 --> 00:35:19.370
deploying Node.js Microservices,
serverless Microservices.

00:35:19.370 --> 00:35:22.180
What have we seen today?

00:35:22.180 --> 00:35:24.370
First, breaking up the monolith.

00:35:24.370 --> 00:35:29.200
So as you've seen, we've built
an entire product made out

00:35:29.200 --> 00:35:32.230
of very, very simple pieces.

00:35:32.230 --> 00:35:36.070
Each of these pieces is a
few files, or even one file

00:35:36.070 --> 00:35:37.510
for some of them.

00:35:37.510 --> 00:35:39.310
Break up the monolith.

00:35:39.310 --> 00:35:42.700
Microservice architectures can
help you build rich products

00:35:42.700 --> 00:35:46.700
from a de-coupled, small,
scalable, maintainable,

00:35:46.700 --> 00:35:49.900
understandable components.

00:35:49.900 --> 00:35:54.600
Second, we showed you
a preview of Node.js

00:35:54.600 --> 00:35:56.750
on App Engine
standard environment.

00:35:56.750 --> 00:36:00.250
This is coming in a few weeks.

00:36:00.250 --> 00:36:02.440
And lastly, what
you should remember

00:36:02.440 --> 00:36:03.970
is that Google
Cloud Platform has

00:36:03.970 --> 00:36:08.950
everything you need to build
serverless Microservices using

00:36:08.950 --> 00:36:10.610
Node.js.

00:36:10.610 --> 00:36:13.630
And personally, I
can't wait to see

00:36:13.630 --> 00:36:16.382
what you will build with
Node.js on App Engine standard

00:36:16.382 --> 00:36:17.465
and all of those products.

